{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgr4FVV2P2FT1xfiGV7oK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014Y2Pa56D3x81ojutFbfWKZ","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"candidates":[{"file":"src/roles/roles.guard.ts","symbol":"RolesGuard.canActivate","start_line":1,"end_line":25,"explanation":"Not in the snippets, so this is inferred. The guard most likely checks the wrong property of the authenticated user, probably request.user.id (or a similar non-role field) against the required role ids instead of request.user.role.id. RoleEnum.admin is 1 and the seeded super admin has user id 1, so only that account passes. Later admins have a different user id, so they get 403 despite having the admin role. UsersController applies this guard with @Roles(RoleEnum.admin) to every endpoint."},{"file":"src/users/users.controller.ts","symbol":"UsersController","start_line":39,"end_line":46,"explanation":"The class-level @Roles(RoleEnum.admin) and @UseGuards(AuthGuard('jwt'), RolesGuard) gate every endpoint here. If the guard's role comparison is wrong, this is where the effect shows up. This is a lower-confidence candidate."},{"file":"src/roles/roles.enum.ts","symbol":"RoleEnum","start_line":1,"end_line":4,"explanation":"RoleEnum.admin is 1, which equals the first user's id. This enables the id-versus-role-id mix-up, so only the first account matches. It is not a defect in itself."}],"fix":{"file":"src/roles/roles.guard.ts","description":"In RolesGuard.canActivate, compare the user's role id, not the user id. Use `roles.map(r => r.toString()).includes(request.user?.role?.id?.toString())`. I haven't confirmed this against the actual guard code."}},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":5049,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},